<section style="margin-top:72px"><span class="label">Comparing makers</span><h2>What to compare, before you commission</h2>
<p>A custom conference table is usually the largest single piece in the room, and makers quote it in different ways. These are the points this page records for every listing, so workshops can be compared on the same terms.</p>
<ul><li><b>Seating and size range.</b> Start from the room, not the catalogue: size the table in the <a href="./#planner">planner</a>, then check the maker builds at that length and width.</li>
<li><b>Material.</b> Solid hardwood can be refinished for decades; veneer and laminate cost less at the outset. Listings state the material plainly.</li>
<li><b>Lead time.</b> Built-to-order work is measured in weeks, and it sets the date the room can be used.</li>
<li><b>Delivery.</b> Long tops need a route into the room — doors, turns, stairs, lift — and, where offered, white-glove placement.</li>
<li><b>Personalisation.</b> Engraved or hand-inlaid marks, cable management and finish, agreed before the build begins.</li></ul>
<p class="note">How listings work: each entry is drawn from the maker's own website and linked for a direct quote. Fargo Woodworks is the featured listing and is labelled as such; it is the workshop behind TableSizer. Other workshops can be added below on the same terms.</p></section>
