// RECEPTION DESK DIMENSIONS
fs.writeFileSync('reception-desk-dimensions.html',page('reception-desk-dimensions.html','Reception Desk Dimensions & Sizes | TableSizer','Reception desk dimensions: 40–42 in counter, 28–34 in work surface, widths for one or two staff, depth and ADA counter requirements.',art(['Guide','Reception desk dimensions','A reception desk has two working heights: a higher counter for standing visitors and a lower surface where staff sit and work. Get those right and the desk works for everyone who approaches it.'],
`<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Standard dimension</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><b>Visitor counter height</b></td><td>40–42 in</td><td>For standing visitors; also gives seated staff some privacy</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Staff work surface</b></td><td>28–34 in</td><td>Seated working height behind the counter</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Width, one station</b></td><td>60–72 in (up to 96 in)</td><td>Room for a monitor, phone and paperwork</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Width, two stations</b></td><td>About 120 in</td><td>Straight desks; L- and U-shaped stations need more depth</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Depth</b></td><td>24–36 in</td><td>Straight desks are commonly 30–36 in deep</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Accessible counter (ADA)</b></td><td>36 in high max; 36 in long min</td><td>Or 30 in long with knee and toe clearance for a forward approach</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>Accessibility is part of the dimension, not an add-on</h2><p>Where the public is served, at least one section of counter must be no higher than 36 inches: at least 36 inches long for a parallel approach, or 30 inches long with knee and toe space for a forward approach (ADA Standards §904.4, U.S. Access Board). California applies a 34-inch maximum to that section, according to OfficeSimpli's guide.</p>
<h2>Leave room around the desk</h2><p>Keep the approach clear so visitors can reach the counter without crossing staff circulation, and allow comfortable passage behind the seated receptionist. If the desk anchors a lobby, plan its width with the room: a single station reads well at 60–72 inches, while larger lobbies and two-person coverage call for 90–120 inches.</p>
<h2>Made-to-measure example</h2><p>For comparison, our featured maker Fargo Woodworks builds its Hartford Reception Desk at 84 or 96 inches, for a single or double station, from $3,200 (fargowoodworks.com). Custom dimensions avoid forcing a lobby to fit a stock footprint.</p>
<p>Sources: <a href="https://www.arcgrove.com/blogs/posts/reception-desk-dimensions">ArcGrove reception desk dimensions guide</a>; <a href="https://www.officesimpli.com/blogs/news/reception-desk-dimensions">OfficeSimpli reception desk dimensions</a>; <a href="https://www.access-board.gov/aba/chapter/ch09/">U.S. Access Board, ADA Chapter 9 (§904.4)</a>.</p>
<p>Planning the meeting rooms behind reception? Start with the <a href="./#planner">conference table planner</a>, or <a href="makers.html">request a quote from a custom maker</a>.</p>`,['boardroom.jpg','Tables and chairs arranged in a bright boardroom','Photograph: Breather, via Unsplash (CC0). Illustrative only.'])));
// CONFERENCE ROOM LAYOUT GUIDE
fs.writeFileSync('conference-room-layout-guide.html',page('conference-room-layout-guide.html','Conference Room Layout Guide: Spacing, Clearance | TableSizer','How to lay out a conference room: clearance behind chairs, space per person, table shapes, display distance and mounting height, with sources.',art(['Guide','Conference room layout & spacing','A good conference room is mostly circulation and sightlines. Size the table for the people, leave room to move behind chairs, and place the display where the farthest seat can still read it.'],
`<h2>Start with people, then the table</h2><p>Allow 30 inches of table edge per person as a minimum, 36 inches for comfort and 42 inches for working meetings (${SRC.pd}). Choose the shape for the room's job: rectangle or boat for discussion, racetrack for a softer footprint, round for small equal-sided meetings, and U or V when a display leads the room. Use the <a href="./#planner">planner</a> to test shapes in your exact room, including the Small huddle, Boardroom, Executive boardroom and Training presets.</p>
<h2>Clearance behind chairs</h2><p>Leave 36–48 inches behind occupied chairs for circulation: 36 inches lets one person pass behind a seated colleague, while 48 inches lets two people pass each other (CubicleByDesign). This planner defaults to 48 inches and allows 42–54 inches. Accessible routes need at least 36 inches (CORADA accessible-meetings guidance).</p>
<h2>Display position and viewing distance</h2><ul><li>Keep at least 36 inches between the table and the display wall where possible, with no seat at the display end (University of Michigan Design Guideline 5.18).</li><li>Size the screen from the farthest viewer: screen height should be at least one-eighth of the distance to the farthest seat, and the closest viewer should sit no nearer than 1.5 times the screen height (Google Room Design Guide).</li><li>Centre the display on the table and keep the bottom of the screen below about 44 inches from the floor (Google); Michigan's guideline aligns display bottom at 49 inches in its rooms, so confirm against your seating and camera.</li></ul>
<h2>A simple layout sequence</h2><ol><li>Measure the room and note doors, columns and the display wall.</li><li>Set headcount and space per person (30/36/42 inches).</li><li>Pick a shape and check seats in the <a href="./#planner">planner</a>.</li><li>Hold 42–48 inches of clearance; add a credenza/display wall allowance if needed.</li><li>Check the farthest seat against your screen size before ordering either.</li></ol>
<p>Sources: ${SRC.pd}; <a href="https://cubiclebydesign.com/size-of-a-conference-room/">CubicleByDesign, conference room size guide</a>; <a href="https://roomdesignguide.withgoogle.com/displays-and-extras/screen">Google Room Design Guide: displays</a>; <a href="https://umaec.umich.edu/desguide/5.0-SBA/SBA5.18.pdf">University of Michigan Design Guideline 5.18 (PDF)</a>; <a href="https://www.corada.com/documents/guide-to-planning-accessible-meetings/meeting-room-layouts-and-considerations">CORADA, accessible meeting layouts</a>.</p>`,['hero-meeting-room.jpg','A long wooden meeting table in a bright room','Photograph: Breather, via Unsplash (CC0). Illustrative only.'])));


// SEAT-COUNT GUIDE (12 / 14 / 16, from SEO-PLAN roadmap 1)
{const faqSeat=[['What size conference table seats 12 people?','At 36 inches per person a 12-seat rectangular table is 15 feet long; at 30 inches per person, 12 feet 6 inches. Width follows the planner rule for that length.'],['What size conference table seats 16 people?','At 36 inches per person a 16-seat rectangular table is 21 feet long; at 30 inches per person, 17 feet 6 inches. Beyond sixteen people, compare a boat or racetrack shape for sightlines.'],['How big should the room be for a 14-person table?','Take the table length and width at your spacing and add 42–48 inches of clearance on every side; the chart shows the minimum room at 36 inches per person with 48 inches of clearance.'],['Should I use 30, 36 or 42 inches per person?','Allow 30 inches per person as a minimum, 36 inches for comfort, and 42 inches where laptops and papers are spread out (Paul Downs; OFW).']];
const rws=[8,10,12,14,16,18,20,22].map(n=>{const a=TP.lengthFor(n,30),b=TP.lengthFor(n,36),c=TP.lengthFor(n,42),w=TP.widthFor(b);return `<tr><td><b>${n}</b></td><td>${ft(a)}</td><td>${ft(b)}</td><td>${ft(c)}</td><td>${w[0]}–${w[1]} in</td><td>${Math.ceil((b+96)/12)} × ${Math.ceil((w[1]+96)/12)} ft</td><td><a class="tlink" href="./?n=${n}&s=36&sh=rectangle&l=${b+96}&w=${w[1]+96}">Plan</a></td></tr>`}).join('');
fs.writeFileSync('conference-table-for-12-people.html',page('conference-table-for-12-people.html','Conference Tables for 12, 14 & 16 People | TableSizer','Conference table sizes for 12, 14 and 16 people: lengths at 30, 36 and 42 inches per person, widths, room sizes and planner links.',art(['Guide','Conference table sizes for 12, 14 and 16 people','Large tables are sized by the same rule as small ones: allow space per person along each side, add one seat at each end, then check the room can hold the result with clearance behind every chair.'],
`<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Seats</th><th>Length @30″</th><th>Length @36″</th><th>Length @42″</th><th>Width</th><th>Min. room (@36″, 48″ clearance)</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody>${rws}</tbody></table></div>
<h2>How to read this chart</h2><p>Lengths come from the planner engine: two people for every full spacing along the length, plus one at each end, rounded up to the nearest 6 inches (${SRC.pd}; ${SRC.ofw}). Width follows length — 36–42 inches up to six feet, rising to 60 inches and beyond for tables over 22 feet — so people at the far end can still see and reach the centre.</p>
<h2>Twelve, fourteen or sixteen?</h2><ul><li><b>12 people:</b> the largest size that still fits many standard boardrooms at comfortable spacing. Start with the <a href="./?n=12&s=36&sh=rectangle">12-seat plan</a>.</li><li><b>14 people:</b> check width as carefully as length; the room, not the table, is usually the limit. Try the <a href="./?n=14&s=36&sh=rectangle">14-seat plan</a>.</li><li><b>16 people:</b> sightlines down a long rectangle get harder, so compare the <a href="boat-shaped-conference-table.html">boat shape</a> and <a href="boardroom-table-sizes.html">boardroom sizes</a> before deciding. Try the <a href="./?n=16&s=36&sh=boat">16-seat boat plan</a>.</li></ul>
<p>Every Plan link opens the planner with that headcount and spacing already set, so you can test your actual room. For other headcounts see the <a href="conference-table-size-chart.html">full size chart (6 to 22 seats)</a> or the <a href="conference-table-dimensions.html">dimensions guide</a>.</p>`,['hero-meeting-room.jpg','A long wooden meeting table with black chairs in a bright room','Photograph: Breather, via Unsplash (CC0). Illustrative only.'],faqSeat)));}
// ROUND GUIDE (roadmap 2)
{const faqRound=[['How many people fit at a 72-inch round table?','At 36 inches per person, a 72-inch round seats 6: its circumference divided by the space per person (Paul Downs).'],['What diameter round table seats 10 people?','At 36 inches per person, allow a 120-inch (10 ft) diameter; at 30 inches per person, 96 inches.'],['Round or rectangle for a small meeting room?','Rectangles seat the most people per foot. Round tables seat their circumference divided by the space per person, and suit smaller, equal-sided conversations where nobody sits at a head.'],['How much room does a round table need?','Add 42–48 inches of clearance on every side of the diameter; the chart shows the minimum square room at 36 inches per person.']];
const rws=[4,6,8,10,12].map(n=>{const a=TP.tableFor('round',n,30),b=TP.tableFor('round',n,36),c=TP.tableFor('round',n,42);return `<tr><td><b>${n}</b></td><td>${ft(a.L)}</td><td>${ft(b.L)}</td><td>${ft(c.L)}</td><td>${Math.ceil((b.L+96)/12)} × ${Math.ceil((b.L+96)/12)} ft</td><td><a class="tlink" href="./?n=${n}&s=36&sh=round&l=${b.L+96}&w=${b.L+96}">Plan</a></td></tr>`}).join('');
fs.writeFileSync('round-conference-table-sizes.html',page('round-conference-table-sizes.html','Round Conference Table Sizes: 4 to 12 Seats | TableSizer','Round conference table sizes by diameter: how many people fit at 48 to 120 inch rounds, at 30, 36 and 42 inches per person.',art(['Guide','Round conference table sizes','A round table seats its circumference divided by the space per person. There is no head of the table, which suits smaller, equal-sided conversations — and caps how many people fit before the diameter outgrows the room.'],
`<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Seats</th><th>Diameter @30″</th><th>Diameter @36″</th><th>Diameter @42″</th><th>Min. room (@36″, 48″ clearance)</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody>${rws}</tbody></table></div>
<h2>The round rule</h2><p>Seats = circumference ÷ space per person, rounded down (${SRC.pd}). That reproduces the published checks used across this site: at 36 inches a 48-inch round seats four and a 96-inch round seats eight. Because the diameter grows with every added seat, rounds are practical roughly from four to twelve people; beyond that, a rectangle or <a href="boat-shaped-conference-table.html">boat shape</a> seats more people in less room.</p>
<h2>Choosing a diameter</h2><ul><li><b>4–6 people:</b> a 48–72 inch round keeps everyone within easy conversation distance.</li><li><b>8–10 people:</b> allow 96–120 inches at comfortable spacing, and check the square room it needs.</li><li><b>12 people:</b> the top of the practical range — compare a rectangle in the <a href="./?n=12&s=36&sh=rectangle">planner</a> before committing.</li></ul>
<p>Test any diameter in your room with the Plan links above, or compare against the <a href="conference-table-dimensions.html">dimensions guide</a> and <a href="conference-table-size-chart.html">size chart by seats</a>.</p>`,['boardroom.jpg','Tables and chairs arranged in a bright boardroom','Photograph: Breather, via Unsplash (CC0). Illustrative only.'],faqRound)));}
// U-SHAPE GUIDE (roadmap 3)
{const faqU=[['How many people fit in a U-shaped layout?','Only the outside edge seats people: two runs plus the head, each divided by the space per person. The chart gives planner-sized layouts for 8 to 20 people.'],['What is a U-shape best for?','Training and presentation rooms: everyone faces inward and toward the open end, where the display or presenter stands. Nobody sits with their back to the screen.'],['How deep is each run of a U-shape?','This planner models each run at 24 inches deep, the depth of training and presentation tables. Allow 42–48 inches of clearance behind the outside chairs.'],['U-shape or V-shape for video calls?','Both leave a wall open for the display and seat the outside edge only. A V angles the two runs toward the display; a U adds a head run across the closed end. Compare both in the planner.']];
const rws=[8,10,12,14,16,20].map(n=>{const t=TP.tableFor('u',n,36),f=TP.footprint('u',t);return `<tr><td><b>${n}</b></td><td>${ft(t.L)}</td><td>${ft(t.W)}</td><td>${ft(f.x)} × ${ft(f.y)}</td><td>${TP.seatsOn('u',t.L,t.W,36)}</td><td>${Math.ceil((f.x+96)/12)} × ${Math.ceil((f.y+96)/12)} ft</td><td><a class="tlink" href="./?n=${n}&s=36&sh=u&wall=1&l=${f.x+96}&w=${f.y+96}">Plan</a></td></tr>`}).join('');
fs.writeFileSync('u-shaped-conference-room-layout.html',page('u-shaped-conference-room-layout.html','U-Shaped Conference Room Layout & Sizes | TableSizer','U-shaped conference room layouts for 8 to 20 people: outside-edge seating, run sizes, room sizes and display-wall guidance.',art(['Guide','U-shaped conference room layouts','A U-shape seats the outside edge only and stays open toward a display or presenter. It trades total seats for sightlines: everyone faces the room and the screen, nobody sits inside the U.'],
`<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Plan for</th><th>Each leg</th><th>Head run</th><th>Overall footprint</th><th>Seats @36″</th><th>Min. room (48″ clearance)</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody>${rws}</tbody></table></div>
<p class="note">Layouts are generated by the planner engine at 36 inches per person; Seats @36″ can exceed the Plan for count because lengths round up to the nearest 6 inches. Each run is modelled 24 inches deep.</p>
<h2>How U seating is counted</h2><p>Seats = the two legs plus the head, each divided by the space per person, outside edge only (${SRC.pd}; ${SRC.ofw}). The inside edge is working space, not seating. Keep the open end toward the display wall, and hold 42–48 inches behind the outside chairs so people can pass (${SRC.os}).</p>
<h2>Planning the room</h2><ol><li>Put the display on the open short wall; the planner Plan links above already allow for a credenza/display wall.</li><li>Size the legs from headcount at 30 inches per person minimum, 36 for comfort.</li><li>Check the farthest seat can read the screen — sizing guidance is in the <a href="conference-room-layout-guide.html">room layout guide</a>.</li><li>For angled sightlines to one display, compare a <a href="./?n=12&s=36&sh=v&wall=1">V-shape plan</a>.</li></ol>
<p>For discussion-led rooms without a presenter, a <a href="conference-table-for-12-people.html">single large table</a> usually seats more people in the same footprint.</p>`,['hero-meeting-room.jpg','A long wooden meeting table in a bright room','Photograph: Breather, via Unsplash (CC0). Illustrative only.'],faqU)));}
// HEIGHT & CLEARANCE GUIDE (roadmap 4)
{const faqH=[['How high is a standard conference table?','Standard seated height is 29–30 inches, paired with standard office chairs. Standing-height tables run about 40–42 inches.'],['How much clearance behind chairs?','Plan 42–48 inches from the table edge to the wall so chairs can be drawn back and people can pass behind them. 36 inches is the accessible-route minimum.'],['How much space per person at the table?','Allow 30 inches of table edge per person as a minimum, 36 inches for comfort, and 42 inches where laptops and papers are spread out (Paul Downs; OFW).'],['How do I check chair and knee clearance?','Keep the underside of the top clear where people sit, and test your actual chairs: arms should slide under the top without forcing shoulders up, and knees should clear any apron or modesty panel. Measure the chairs you own rather than assuming a standard.']];
fs.writeFileSync('conference-table-height-clearance.html',page('conference-table-height-clearance.html','Conference Table Height & Chair Clearance | TableSizer','Standard conference table height (29-30 in), clearance behind chairs (42-48 in), knee and chair-arm checks, with planner links.',art(['Guide','Conference table height & clearance','Length gets the attention, but height and clearance decide whether a table is comfortable for an hour. Three numbers do most of the work: top height, space per person, and the gap behind occupied chairs.'],
`<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Dimension</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><b>Seated table height</b></td><td>29–30 in</td><td>Standard height, paired with standard office chairs</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Standing-height table</b></td><td>About 40–42 in</td><td>For stand-up and perch-stool meetings</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Space per person</b></td><td>30 / 36 / 42 in</td><td>Minimum / comfortable / laptops and papers spread out</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Clearance behind chairs</b></td><td>42–48 in</td><td>Chairs drawn back, people passing behind; this planner allows 42–54 in</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Accessible route</b></td><td>36 in minimum</td><td>Minimum clear route; do not plan a main circulation path below it</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>Height: 29–30 inches is the standard</h2><p>Most conference tables are built 29–30 inches high, matched to standard office chairs. Standing-height tables at about 40–42 inches suit short, active meetings. Whichever you choose, keep the underside clear where people sit: an apron, beam or modesty panel that drops into knee space is felt within minutes, so confirm its depth with your maker before the build.</p>
<h2>Chair clearance is a room dimension</h2><p>Allow 42–48 inches from the table edge to the wall so chairs can be drawn back and people can pass behind seated colleagues (${SRC.os}). That clearance is already inside every room size on this site: pick a table from the <a href="conference-table-size-chart.html">size chart</a>, then check the room column, or set clearance yourself in the planner (42, 48 or 54 inches).</p>
<h2>A five-minute chair check</h2><ol><li>Measure your chair's arm height and seat height as it will actually be used.</li><li>Arms should slide under the 29–30 inch top without lifting the sitter's shoulders.</li><li>Sit at a sample place setting: 36 inches of edge per person is comfortable; 30 is the minimum.</li><li>Push the chair back and walk behind it — that is the 42–48 inch test, in person.</li></ol>
<p>Then size the full table in the <a href="./?n=10&s=36&sh=rectangle&c=48">planner at 48-inch clearance</a>, and read the <a href="conference-room-layout-guide.html">room layout guide</a> for display distance and circulation.</p>`,['wood-table-detail.jpg','A wooden table top set against a white wall','Photograph: Crew, via Unsplash (CC0). Illustrative only.'],faqH)));}
// BOAT GUIDE (roadmap 5)
{const faqB=[['What is a boat-shaped conference table?','A table that is wider in the middle than at the ends. This planner seats it like a rectangle and models the centre belly at about 6 inches wider than the ends.'],['Boat or rectangle: which seats more?','They seat the same for a given length under the planner rules: two people for every full spacing along the length, plus one at each end. Choose the boat for sightlines, the rectangle for maximum simplicity.'],['What size boat-shaped table seats 12 people?','At 36 inches per person, 15 feet long; at 30 inches per person, 12 feet 6 inches. Allow for the wider centre when checking room width.'],['When should I choose a boat shape?','On longer tables (roughly 12 seats and up) where people must see and hear each other down the table; the wider middle angles sightlines inward. For a formal board setting compare boardroom sizes too.']];
const rws=[8,10,12,14,16,18,20,22].map(n=>{const a=TP.tableFor('boat',n,30),b=TP.tableFor('boat',n,36),c=TP.tableFor('boat',n,42);return `<tr><td><b>${n}</b></td><td>${ft(a.L)}</td><td>${ft(b.L)}</td><td>${ft(c.L)}</td><td>${b.W} in ends · ${b.W+6} in centre</td><td><a class="tlink" href="./?n=${n}&s=36&sh=boat&l=${b.L+96}&w=${b.W+6+96}">Plan</a></td></tr>`}).join('');
fs.writeFileSync('boat-shaped-conference-table.html',page('boat-shaped-conference-table.html','Boat-Shaped Conference Table Sizes & Guide | TableSizer','Boat-shaped conference table sizes for 8 to 22 people: lengths, centre widths, sightlines, and when to choose boat vs rectangle.',art(['Guide','Boat-shaped conference tables','A boat shape is a rectangle widened at the middle. It seats the same headcount per foot, but the curve turns people slightly toward each other — which is why it earns its place on longer tables.'],
`<div class="tablewrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Seats</th><th>Length @30″</th><th>Length @36″</th><th>Length @42″</th><th>Width @36″ (ends · centre)</th><th></th></tr></thead><tbody>${rws}</tbody></table></div>
<h2>How boat seating is counted</h2><p>Exactly like a rectangle: two people for every full spacing along the length, plus one at each end (${SRC.pd}). The planner models the centre belly at about 6 inches wider than the ends, so check room width against the centre figure, not the ends. Width still follows length overall — see <a href="conference-table-dimensions.html">standard dimensions</a>.</p>
<h2>Boat vs rectangle vs racetrack</h2><ul><li><b>Rectangle:</b> most seats per foot, simplest to build and extend.</li><li><b>Boat:</b> wider middle improves sightlines down a long table; the usual choice from about 12 seats up.</li><li><b>Racetrack:</b> rounded ends soften the footprint while keeping straight-side seating.</li></ul>
<p>Compare all three at your headcount in the planner — start with the <a href="./?n=16&s=36&sh=boat">16-seat boat plan</a> — and check <a href="boardroom-table-sizes.html">boardroom sizes</a> if the room is a formal board setting. For 12–16 seat lengths side by side, see <a href="conference-table-for-12-people.html">tables for 12, 14 and 16 people</a>.</p>`,['fargo-timberline.jpg','Fargo Woodworks Timberline conference table with black chairs','Photograph: Fargo Woodworks, from fargowoodworks.com. Illustrative of a large hardwood table.'],faqB)));}

